`urllib.request.Request` builds a request object and sends nothing.
`urlopen(req)` is the send (INV-gujoh). The net_send row on Request was
wrong and was kept, because deleting it made every `Request(...)` an
unclassified call into an unenumerated module. The coverage gate
(_uncatalogued_external_modules) then withholds the clean verdict: all
18 of hypergumbo's own self-claims went inconclusive.

This change enumerates the module first, per the 2026-08-15 rule
(docs/surveys/python-stdlib-module-io-enumeration.md, new addendum).
Its 46 own public callables were probed and read one by one.
- net_send: urlopen and urlretrieve (already rowed); ftpwrapper, NEW
  (its __init__ connects and sends USER/PASS).
- net_recv: localhost, thishost, NEW (gethostbyname: the DNS ruling,
  WI-dozul).
- env_read: getproxies, getproxies_environment, proxy_bypass,
  proxy_bypass_environment, ProxyHandler (proxies=None reads the
  environment), build_opener (always adds a ProxyHandler), URLopener,
  FancyURLopener, all NEW.
- fs_write: urlcleanup, NEW (unlinks urlretrieve's temp files).
- none: Request, OpenerDirector, install_opener, the other 22 handler
  classes, and the string helpers.
Then urllib.request is declared complete, dated 2026-09-24, and the
Request row is deleted.

NOT CLAIMED: urllib.request.OpenerDirector.open sends and carries no
row. It is its own slot, which is not declared complete.

